# <<scenario_name>>

A glossogen scenario in its own package. Generated by `glossogen new-scenario`,
and runnable as generated: two agents relay a code word over a metered link, and
a round succeeds when the word gets across.

## Setup

```bash
python -m venv .venv && source .venv/bin/activate
pip install -e ".[testing]"
cp .env.example .env      # then put your ANTHROPIC_API_KEY in it
```

The install is what registers the scenario: the entry point in `pyproject.toml`
is read from installed metadata, so an uninstalled source tree resolves to
nothing.

`pyproject.toml` pins glossogen to `<<glossogen_ref>>`, the ref `new-scenario`
was given. That defaults to the release matching the version of the glossogen it
ran from, so if you generated this from a branch or a commit install, it names
the release that install is ahead of rather than what you are running. Change it
to your own ref if so.

## Check it

```bash
glossogen validate .                          # the contract, no install, no API key
pytest                                        # the round loop, no API key needed
```

`validate` reads this package's own `pyproject.toml`, so it works before you have
installed anything and every time you edit. It also checks the package itself:
that `package-data` covers the prompts and presets, that the entry-point group
names the contract version this glossogen reads, that the package `__init__` is
empty, and that nothing else already answers to
`<<scenario_name>>`.

Once installed, `glossogen validate <<scenario_name>>` runs the same contract checks
by name. That is the form for CI, where the package is installed anyway.

## Run it

```bash
glossogen run <<scenario_name>> \
  --config knobs_default \
  --model claude-haiku-4-5-20251001 --provider anthropic \
  --runs-dir ./runs
```

`--config` takes the name of a preset this package ships, or a path to a JSON
file of your own. Add `round_count=5` or any other knob as trailing `key=value`
arguments to override the preset for one run.

## Look at it

```bash
glossogen serve --runs-dir ./runs --port 8000 --ui-port 3000
```

The web UI at <http://localhost:3000> lists the run, with the message timeline,
each agent's reasoning, and the round verdicts. `--ui-port` needs Docker; drop it
to serve the API alone.

## Making it yours

The generated scenario works so that you can change one thing at a time and see
what breaks. In rough order:

1. `prompts/*.jinja` and `scenario_description` — what the agents are told.
2. `ids.py` and `team_declaration.py` — who exists, what channels they have, and
   which tools each may call.
3. `world.py` — what changes during a round, and what the agents are notified of.
4. `judge_round_result` in `scenario.py` — what counts as success. This is what
   the `round_success` metric reads.
5. `knobs.py` and `knobs_default.json` — what a run can vary.

`__init__.py` stays empty, and `events.py` imports only from
`glossogen.models.event_base`: both are load-bearing for discovery.

Full guide: <https://github.com/agencyenterprise/GlossoGen/blob/main/docs/creating-a-scenario.md>
